Operator · Form
$and · { $and: [ filterA, filterB ] } (implicit when conditions are on different fields)
$or · { $or: [ filterA, filterB ] }
$nor · { $nor: [ filterA, filterB ] } (none match)
$not · { field: { $not: { $gt: 10 } } } (negate a sub-expression)
db.users.find({
$or: [
{ age: { $lt: 18 } },
{ age: { $gt: 65 } }
]
})
db.orders.find({
status: "paid",
$and: [
{ total: { $gte: 50 } },
{ at: { $gte: ISODate("2026-05-20") } }
]
})
Most simple AND queries don't need $and explicitly — multiple keys at the top level are AND-ed automatically:
db.orders.find({ status: "paid", total: { $gte: 50 } }) // implicit AND
Use explicit $and when you need multiple conditions on the same field:
db.orders.find({
$and: [
{ total: { $gte: 50 } },
{ total: { $lte: 200 } }
]
})
// or equivalently
db.orders.find({ total: { $gte: 50, $lte: 200 } })

This one trips beginners. Consider:
db.students.insertMany([
{ name: "A", scores: [{ subject: "math", grade: 80 }, { subject: "eng", grade: 60 }] },
{ name: "B", scores: [{ subject: "math", grade: 50 }, { subject: "eng", grade: 90 }] },
])
"Find students with a single subject scoring math ≥ 70":
// WRONG — matches students where any subject is math AND any subject ≥ 70
db.students.find({ "scores.subject": "math", "scores.grade": { $gte: 70 } })
// matches both A and B (B has eng=90 ≥ 70 + math elsewhere)
// RIGHT — both conditions must hold for the SAME array element
db.students.find({
scores: { $elemMatch: { subject: "math", grade: { $gte: 70 } } }
})
// matches only A
Use $elemMatch whenever multiple conditions must apply to the same array element.

$expr lets you reference other fields in the same document or use aggregation operators inside a find filter:
// orders where total > 10 × number of items
db.orders.find({
$expr: { $gt: ["$total", { $multiply: [10, { $size: "$items" }] }] }
})
Useful for cross-field predicates without a full aggregation pipeline. $expr doesn't always use indexes the same way; check .explain() (Post 04).

db.places.createIndex({ location: "2dsphere" })
db.places.find({
location: {
$near: {
$geometry: { type: "Point", coordinates: [-0.1, 51.5] },
$maxDistance: 5000 // metres
}
}
})
db.places.find({
location: {
$geoWithin: {
$geometry: { type: "Polygon", coordinates: [[ [...], [...], ... ]] }
}
}
})
Operators: $near, $nearSphere, $geoWithin, $geoIntersects. Indexes: 2dsphere (spherical), 2d (legacy planar).
Skip the deep dive unless your app needs it — full coverage in dedicated docs.

db.students.find(
{ name: "A" },
{ name: 1, scores: { $elemMatch: { subject: "math" } } }
)
// returns only the math entry from scores

db.students.find(
{ "scores.subject": "math" },
{ "scores.$": 1 }
)
// returns scores array containing only the matched element

Sort by a unique-enough field (often _id or a timestamp). Use the last seen value as a cursor for the next page.
// page 1
const first = await db.collection('orders').find({ status: "paid" })
.sort({ at: -1 })
.limit(20)
.toArray();
const last = first[first.length - 1];
// page 2
const next = await db.collection('orders').find({
status: "paid",
at: { $lt: last.at } // strictly less than last seen
})
.sort({ at: -1 })
.limit(20)
.toArray();
Always O(limit) per page — independent of how deep into the list you've gone.
For non-unique sort keys (multiple rows with the same at), tiebreak on _id:
{
$or: [
{ at: { $lt: last.at } },
{ at: last.at, _id: { $lt: last._id } }
]
}
With a compound index on { at: -1, _id: -1 }, this stays O(limit).
Trick: any "Load more" / infinite-scroll UI must use range pagination. Server-side render-time pagination (offset for SEO) tolerates .skip() only up to a few hundred items.

What · How
Equals · { field: value }
Range · { field: { $gt: a, $lte: b } }
One of · { field: { $in: [a, b] } }
Multi-cond OR · { $or: [{ a: 1 }, { b: 2 }] }
Field present · { field: { $exists: true } }
Array contains x · { tags: "x" }
Array contains all · { tags: { $all: ["x", "y"] } }
Array elem multi-cond · { arr: { $elemMatch: { a: 1, b: { $gt: 2 } } } }
Array size N · { arr: { $size: N } }
Regex (anchored, indexable) · { s: /^prefix/ }
Cross-field compare · { $expr: { $gt: ["$a", "$b"] } }
Project include · { name: 1, email: 1, _id: 0 }
Project array slice · { arr: { $slice: 5 } }
Project matched array elem · { "arr.$": 1 } (with matched filter)
Pagination at scale · range (< lastSeen) not .skip()
Force index · .hint({ index })
Tag for profiler · .comment("...")
Timeout safety · .maxTimeMS(500)
Distinct · db.c.distinct("field", filter?)
